# Line endings.
#
# This repo is authored on Windows but its scripts must run on Linux too -- the
# shipped .devcontainer, WSL, and CI runners all execute them. A shell script
# committed with CRLF fails at exec time with "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M",
# so these types are pinned to LF in the repository regardless of the author's
# core.autocrlf setting.
#
# Catch-all FIRST (later lines override it): every text file gets an explicit eol, so no path is left to the
# checkout's core.autocrlf. MEASURED 2026-10-11 (CI run 38178385999, windows-latest): .gitignore, LICENSE,
# SETUP_PROMPT.txt and tests/guard_corpus/game-root.txt matched no rule, checked out CRLF under the runner's
# autocrlf=true, and build_release_zip.py -- whose `git status` runs with core.autocrlf=false so .gitattributes
# alone decides -- saw them as modified and refused the release build as a dirty tree.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
# Extensionless bash launchers.
tools/skydoctor text eol=lf

# Windows-side helpers stay CRLF.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Never touch binaries.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.bsa   binary
*.esp   binary
*.esm   binary
*.esl   binary
*.nif   binary
*.xwm   binary

# Extensionless test shims are shell scripts too. Without this they inherit the
# author's core.autocrlf and check out as CRLF, which dies on a Linux runner with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" -- the exact failure the block above prevents
# for *.sh. Caught by a fresh-clone check, not in CI.
tests/bin/* text eol=lf

# Text types owned by scripts/gen_agent_trees.py and its agent/ source (*.sh and *.json are
# pinned above). The generator writes LF and its drift gate compares bytes, so an autocrlf
# checkout must not rewrite these. Pinned BY EXTENSION, never by directory: a later
# `agent/** text` line would override `*.png binary` and strip bytes from any image in a skill.
*.md               text eol=lf
*.example          text eol=lf
*.jsonl            text eol=lf

# The frozen Codex hook definitions and their ledger (Plan 4 Task 8): the freeze gate hashes LF bytes, and
# *.toml / *.sha256 are not pinned above. A path rule for one text-only folder, not an extension rule.
agent/targets/codex/* text eol=lf

# Generated Codex tree (Plan 4 Task 10). The generator writes LF and its drift gate compares bytes, but
# `*.ps1` is pinned CRLF above and `*.toml` / `*.rules` are pinned nowhere. A PATH rule, placed after
# `*.ps1` so it wins, for a folder that holds only text (no images): not a global `*.ps1 eol=lf`, which
# would flip every Windows helper. .agents/ holds skills, which may carry images, so it is pinned by
# extension only (`*.md` above), never by directory.
.codex/** text eol=lf

# Plan 6 Task 12: the guard manifest is compared byte for byte (scripts/gen-guard-hashes.py --check).
GUARD-FILES.sha256 text eol=lf

# The 3.x -> 4.0 migration fixtures are a real v3.9.1 install, compared byte for byte (T9). Text only.
tests/fixtures/migrate-3x/** text eol=lf

# Read by Linux tools (the devcontainer build, CI's setup-node/setup-python, pip, javac in the container): an
# autocrlf checkout must not rewrite them. MEASURED 2026-10-06: master's Dockerfile blob is LF but a Windows
# checkout had 79 CRLF lines, which breaks every backslash-continued RUN line in a Linux build.
.devcontainer/**   text eol=lf
*.lock             text eol=lf
*.toml             text eol=lf
*.java             text eol=lf
.node-version      text eol=lf
.python-version    text eol=lf
requirements*.txt  text eol=lf

# This file itself: an autocrlf checkout must not rewrite it either (a mutation anchor reads its lines).
.gitattributes     text eol=lf
